  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Rose-Ink Postcard",
    "overview": "Every tweet is a postcard: a short note on a soft white card, sitting on a quiet paper-gray table. The system is friendly, soft and tidy. Shapes are rounded, surfaces float slightly, and one warm rose ink marks everything you can write, send, like or follow. The rose works because it is the only warm color on screen. Everything else is a neutral run from Paper to Night Ink.\n\nDensity is calm. The whole app lives in a single 672px reading column, and cards stack with 12px gaps. Each card has generous 16px insets, so even a feed of 140-character posts reads like a stack of separate notes rather than a scrolling log. Craft shows at the edges: the 1px press on buttons, hairline rings on cards, toasts that slide in and pause under the pointer, and deleted tweets that fade and shrink away instead of vanishing.\n\nThe identity is Tweetable's own. It borrows Twitter's mechanics (short posts, replies, likes, profiles) but not its blue, its layout or its iconography. The signature is the typographic `{Tweet}able` wordmark: Roboto in curly braces, with \"Tweet\" written in rose.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One warm accent (Rose Ink) on an otherwise neutral, slightly cool palette.",
      "White cards with a 16px radius on a Paper canvas, lifted by a hairline ring and the faintest shadow.",
      "Pill-shaped buttons and avatars everywhere; nothing interactive has a sharp corner.",
      "Montserrat headings over Inter body text: geometric warmth on top, neutral readability underneath.",
      "A single centered 672px column on every screen size.",
      "Soft, short motion (150–300ms) used only for feedback, never decoration."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Warm Color Rule",
        "body": "Rose Ink and its ramp are the only warm hues in the product. Don't introduce a second accent (no blue links, no green success). Success is already Rose Glow on Night Ink.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Red Means Gone Rule",
        "body": "The error ramp appears only for destructive actions (delete) and failures (validation, alert toasts). Never use it for emphasis, badges or decoration.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Contrast Debt Rule",
        "body": "Three incumbent pairings fall below WCAG AA for small text: white on Rose Ink (4.16:1, used on 14px button labels), Muted Gray on Card White (4.29:1), and Muted Gray on Paper (3.83:1, used in the footer). Don't extend these pairings to new places. When a new label must be rose with white text, put it on Rose Ink Deep (5.78:1).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Quiet Uppercase Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase is reserved for 12px tracked labels in Muted Gray. Headings and buttons stay in sentence case.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Braces Belong to the Logo Rule",
        "body": "Curly braces and Roboto appear only in the wordmark. Don't reuse them as a decorative motif or as a heading font.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Postcard Rests Rule",
        "body": "Cards are on the table, not hovering over it. A card never gets more than the resting lift. Hover feedback on a tweet card is a faint Paper tint, not a bigger shadow.",
